Well, yes, that too, but I thought perhaps someone had told you, or
you'd read it somewhere, and I didn't want to insult you by telling you
something you already knew.

Potions will dilute when they get wet (remember that holy water and
unholy water aren't very wet). 

You can get things wet by dipping them into any body of water. You can't
dip when you're next to the water (probably because NH is not real life
and you can't kneel beside it), or hovering above the water, but
dipping when you're on or even in the water is feasible.

On early levels, your usual dipping place will be fountains. They tend
to dry up after a few dips, and they can spawn water demons and snakes;
moats, which you'll encounter later, can contain nasty monsters that
grab you and pull you under.

If you dip an already diluted potion into water, it will become plain
water.

Some potions become water when you dip a unicorn horn into them. First
dip something you don't mind losing (not another potion) in case it's
polymorph; you don't want to be stuck with, for instance, a tin opener
instead of your unicorn horn.

Cancelling potions turns most into water and some into fruit juice;
booze, oil and fruit juice just become uncursed. 

